WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:03.250
Film Courage: What’s been the easiest year
you’ve had as a filmmaker and then what

00:00:03.250 --> 00:00:04.530
has been your toughest year?

00:00:04.530 --> 00:00:14.110
Alex Munoz, Filmmaker and Founder of FYI Films:
I think my easiest year was 2000 when I booked

00:00:14.110 --> 00:00:18.660
my first film RIOT the Showtime film.

00:00:18.660 --> 00:00:21.750
I was brought in under tragic circumstances.

00:00:21.750 --> 00:00:26.300
The guy who wrote it was Joseph Vasquez.

00:00:26.300 --> 00:00:31.020
He passed away and they were looking for a
new director and it turns out that most of

00:00:31.020 --> 00:00:40.750
the producers went to USC and there was buzz
about my thesis film called Por vida and I

00:00:40.750 --> 00:00:44.770
mean I did my first movie right when I got
out of film school and I got a commercial

00:00:44.770 --> 00:00:52.490
agent and I was doing commercials and my other
film got into Sundance (it was such a year).

00:00:52.490 --> 00:01:00.550
And yes there is some happenstance in luck…I
don’t like to call it luck because someone

00:01:00.550 --> 00:01:06.020
died but the writer/director passed away that
needed someone to direct his segment and I

00:01:06.020 --> 00:01:11.259
had to come in and change it because the way
he wrote it (he was from New York, a brilliant

00:01:11.259 --> 00:01:15.179
writer director from New York (New York Rican)
and I had to make it more LA.

00:01:15.179 --> 00:01:20.729
But that was a…I was thinking wow…how
did this happen?

00:01:20.729 --> 00:01:24.749
I’m directing a film for Showtime and I
just got out of film school.

00:01:24.749 --> 00:01:25.890
Is this really happening?

00:01:25.890 --> 00:01:30.799
I was going to Berlin and going to all of
these film festivals.

00:01:30.799 --> 00:01:35.360
But the point of ignition was me getting that
Showtime movie.

00:01:35.360 --> 00:01:38.149
And then…the worst year was probably the
year I did.

00:01:38.149 --> 00:01:44.909
I don’t even like looking at the film because
the producer were not honest.

00:01:44.909 --> 00:01:49.240
They cut out eight core scenes and they said
we’re going to shoot these scenes in LA

00:01:49.240 --> 00:01:52.669
and I said Yes let’s shoot the scenes because
if you don’t shoot these eight scenes that

00:01:52.669 --> 00:01:54.439
story wont make sense.

00:01:54.439 --> 00:01:55.439
We wrapped the film.

00:01:55.439 --> 00:01:58.179
We got to LA and they said we are not shooting
those scenes.

00:01:58.179 --> 00:02:01.340
They never planned to shoot the scenes.

00:02:01.340 --> 00:02:07.950
They did shoot some additional new scenes
and I found out and I told them You can’t

00:02:07.950 --> 00:02:09.229
do this.

00:02:09.229 --> 00:02:12.690
I am DGA [Directors Guild of America] and
I will report this to the union and my DP

00:02:12.690 --> 00:02:16.620
said he would report it also to his union.

00:02:16.620 --> 00:02:20.170
And they said if you go to the union we will
just drag your name through the mud.

00:02:20.170 --> 00:02:21.170
I said Go ahead.

00:02:21.170 --> 00:02:22.629
Nothing is going to come up.

00:02:22.629 --> 00:02:29.269
So I had to go to my union and fight just
to shoot some of the new scenes.

00:02:29.269 --> 00:02:36.150
I almost reached the point of surrender where
I said to myself I worked really hard to get

00:02:36.150 --> 00:02:37.150
into USC.

00:02:37.150 --> 00:02:42.829
I worked really hard to come up for the money
to pay you to get my master’s degree.

00:02:42.829 --> 00:02:43.920
Why am I doing this?

00:02:43.920 --> 00:02:49.349
Why am I going to spend six months of my life
on a film only to have it re-edited and have

00:02:49.349 --> 00:02:51.319
stuff filmed behind my back.

00:02:51.319 --> 00:02:52.879
This is BS.

00:02:52.879 --> 00:02:58.010
But what I did instead is I just refused to
stand under the banner called victim.

00:02:58.010 --> 00:03:02.359
I’m going to learn from this and now I am
a fiercely independent filmmaker.

00:03:02.359 --> 00:03:06.790
I’ve got to have final cut and the films
that have done well for me (my most award-winning

00:03:06.790 --> 00:03:10.569
films) are films where I had final say in
the creative cut because I know what I want

00:03:10.569 --> 00:03:12.489
and I am clear about my vision.

00:03:12.489 --> 00:03:19.090
But it was a bad year because I wanted to
claim the Alan Smithee clause which is if

00:03:19.090 --> 00:03:23.099
you don’t like the film, if it deviates
too much from your vision you don’t have

00:03:23.099 --> 00:03:26.650
to have your name on it but if I took my name
off of it I would lose all of the residuals

00:03:26.650 --> 00:03:28.959
and I needed the money.

00:03:28.959 --> 00:03:36.430
Not only did the film not do well it (it didn’t
tank, it was just very, very lukewarm and

00:03:36.430 --> 00:03:43.530
then I kind of got a little blacklisted for
running to the union and people were…I became

00:03:43.530 --> 00:03:44.530
a distant outpost.

00:03:44.530 --> 00:03:49.769
No one really wanted to touch me saying this
guy went to the DGA like a little baby and

00:03:49.769 --> 00:03:52.540
people didn’t really want to talk to me
or hire me.

00:03:52.540 --> 00:03:56.310
That was a very tough year for me.

00:03:56.310 --> 00:04:03.689
But I recovered and you know we survive, we
survive and we overcome and we keep going.

00:04:03.689 --> 00:04:07.840
My auntie used to say ‘Onward Ever, Keep
Going.’

00:04:07.840 --> 00:04:10.440
There is a Japanese proverb.

00:04:10.440 --> 00:04:14.840
It’s Fall Down Eight Times, Get Up Nine
Times.

00:04:14.840 --> 00:04:18.000
I have that above my wall, above my desk in
my office.

